00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.800
<v Valdo>Good morning.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:40.400
<v Orlando>Okay, today we're going to talk about negatives.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:45.079
<v Orlando>And as you listen to the dialogue,
Brazilians have this wonderful,

00:00:45.079 --> 00:00:50.550
<v Orlando>wonderful intonation that goes with using
the word "no" and just listen to it.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:51.800
<v Orlando>It's just a neat flow.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:55.613
<v Orlando>It's one of my favorite sounds when
people go, "No, no, no, no, no, no,

00:00:55.613 --> 00:00:59.640
<v Orlando>no." We're going to be talking about
being invited to parties and not going.

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:04.050
<v Orlando>The basic rule is that Brazilians are
going to repeat the word "no" three times.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.050
<v Michelle>Yes.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:05.890
<v Valdo>Yes.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:06.550
<v Michelle>At least.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.300
<v Valdo>No, no, no, no.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.050
<v Michelle>No, no, no.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.800
<v Orlando>So they're going to say no to negate the question.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:13.800
<v Orlando>Do you want to go?

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:14.550
<v Valdo>No.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.800
<v Orlando>Then they're going to say no a second time
to negate the verb, I don't go, so no I don't

